WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.962
This exercise asks you to find notes that need follow-up.

00:00:02.962 --> 00:00:08.592
The JSON document contains a Boolean flag, but the key name is missing from the starter condition.

00:00:08.592 --> 00:00:16.407
Look at the earlier JSONB example and identify which key stores that follow-up information.

00:00:16.407 --> 00:00:20.212
Replace the placeholder with the quoted key name, then run the query.

00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:26.762
Inspect the returned notes and confirm that each one represents a true follow-up flag.

00:00:26.762 --> 00:00:30.977
Notice that extracting JSON as text is different from comparing a Boolean.

00:00:30.977 --> 00:00:33.692
The example handles that conversion for you.

00:00:33.692 --> 00:00:37.192
Focus on the pattern: choose the JSON key, extract the value, cast it when necessary,

00:00:37.192 --> 00:00:40.352
then use the result in a condition.

00:00:40.352 --> 00:00:44.597
You do not need to memorize every JSON operator yet.

00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:49.082
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:54.422
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:54.422 --> 00:00:58.272
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:58.272 --> 00:01:02.752
In a real setting such as an analyst exploring operational records before building a report,

00:01:02.752 --> 00:01:10.707
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:12.612
before discussing syntax.

00:01:12.612 --> 00:01:17.548
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
